    id: "b353316083a99936061a",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Why Moscow is staying tight-lipped on Trump's energy ceasefire",
    summary: "While Ukraine's Volodymyr Zelensky suggests he would sign up to a ceasefire on energy infrastructure, Russia has said little.",
    preview: "On Sunday, US President Donald Trump announced that Russia and Ukraine had agreed a ceasefire on strikes on energy infrastructure \"effective immediately\". Ukraine's Volodymyr Zelensky suggested it was \"news for me\", while Russia has not commented publicly. So who is saying what? Donald Trump says an energy ceasefire has been agreed. Kyiv asks: Has it? It is willing to sign up â if Russia does. Russia is saying: Very little.",
    body: "On Sunday, US President Donald Trump announced that Russia and Ukraine had agreed a ceasefire on strikes on energy infrastructure \"effective immediately\". Ukraine's Volodymyr Zelensky suggested it was \"news for me\", while Russia has not commented publicly.\n\nSo who is saying what?\n\nDonald Trump says an energy ceasefire has been agreed.\n\nKyiv asks: Has it? It is willing to sign up â if Russia does.\n\nRussia is saying: Very little.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin's spokesman Dmitry Peskov was asked whether such a ceasefire had been agreed during his daily conference call with journalists.\n\n\"Contacts are continuing,\" Peskov replied.\n\n\"As soon as we have anything to say about this, we will definitely inform you.\"\n\nPeskov may not have had much to say on the matter, but the pro-Kremlin media has.\n\nIt has dubbed President Trump's announcement of an energy ceasefire \"a gift\" for Moscow.\n\n\"Of course we benefit,\" pro-government news outlet Moskovsky Komsomolets concluded.\n\n\"It means that all Russia has to do is not destroy Ukraine's last remaining oil refinery.\n\n\"That has already been attacked several times. It's barely functioning anyway.\n\n\"But Kyiv won't be allowed to hit the multitude of refineries we have.\n\n\"In effect it's a one-way deal that benefits us. That's why no one here has officially rejected it.\"\n\nIn other words, Moscow believes Trump's announcement ties Ukraine's hands, not Russia's.\n\nIn recent months, long-range Ukrainian drone strikes on Russian oil facilities have caused extensive damage, as well as fuel shortages across the country.\n\nShould such attacks continue, expect the Russians to claim that Kyiv is harming US economic interests â as well as the Republican Party's chances in the midterm elections in November.\n\nRussian officials will argue that the strikes undermine the recent Trump-Putin fuel deal for the US to import Russian diesel.\n\nTrump has claimed the agreement will benefit US consumers, although there is widespread scepticism that the volumes involved will do much to lower fuel prices.\n\nStill, the Kremlin will be only too happy if Ukrainian drone strikes on oil facilities spark fury in the White House and curtail US support for Kyiv.\n\nMoscow would welcome Washington cutting off intelligence sharing to Ukraine and weapons sales to Europe that are destined for Kyiv.\n\nIt has already delighted to hear Trump declaring: \"I think it's time for Ukraine to get a new president.\"\n\nUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky has called for a 60-day Russian ceasefire to allow for presidential elections in Ukraine.\n\nMoscow's reaction?\n\n\"The Kremlin, the president, has said many times before, we don't want a ceasefire, we want a lasting peace, and we're willing to do whatever's possible for that,\" Peskov said.\n\n\"Whatever's possible\" does not include a 60-day ceasefire, or a 30-day ceasefire, or anything close to that.\n\nOn Monday, the Kremlin proposed a one-day cessation of hostilities for Ukraine to hold elections.\n\nThat will not be seen in Ukraine, or in the West, as a serious offer.\n\nIt comes as no surprise though.\n\nPutin has shown little desire to halt the fighting.\n\nRecent statements, speeches and signals from Russia's leader suggest that, with winter approaching, the Kremlin is counting on a continued campaign of Russian strikes to significantly increase the pressure on Ukraine.\n\nOn Monday, Russia's Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said Ukraine does not deserve the \"respite\" a ceasefire would bring.\n\nThe Kremlin has claimed to seek \"lasting peace\" but right now it is continuing the fighting.\n\nOn Monday, Zelensky claimed that Russia had hit several energy targets across Ukraine during the night and into the morning.\n\n\"This window of opportunity should be used to knock Ukraine back to the Stone Age and force it to accept peace on Russia's terms,\" the pro-government Moskovsky Komsomolets has written.\n\nThe Kremlin's objective hasn't changed: Ukraine's capitulation.\n\nMoscow seems to believe that the diesel deal with Trump and the US president's growing impatience with Zelensky will help Russia secure its goal.",
