    id: "a8912cd90749db131cf3",
    cat: "ai",
    title: "Hundreds of supermarkets in France hit by ‘unprecedented’ cyberattack",
    summary: "Hackers raise temperatures in supermarket fridges, sparking fires and spoiling food in 'large-scale' cyberattack.",
    preview: "Hackers raise temperatures in fridges at hundreds of supermarkets, sparking fires and spoiling food in ‘large-scale’ cyberattack. Cyberattack on French supermarkets causes fires, millions in losses A cyberattack on refrigeration systems at hundreds of supermarkets in France has raised the temperature of fridges and freezers to as high as 50 degrees Celsius (122F), spoiling food and causing fires at several stores. Top retailers Intermarche, Cooperative U, E Leclerc and others confirmed the “unprecedented” cyberattack on Monday.",
    body: "Hackers raise temperatures in fridges at hundreds of supermarkets, sparking fires and spoiling food in ‘large-scale’ cyberattack.\n\nCyberattack on French supermarkets causes fires, millions in losses\n\nA cyberattack on refrigeration systems at hundreds of supermarkets in France has raised the temperature of fridges and freezers to as high as 50 degrees Celsius (122F), spoiling food and causing fires at several stores.\n\nTop retailers Intermarche, Cooperative U, E Leclerc and others confirmed the “unprecedented” cyberattack on Monday.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 4 items\n\n- list 1 of 4Stolen Renoir paintings found behind wall in France following arrests\n\n- list 2 of 4France school shooting: Police detain pupil after two injured\n\n- list 3 of 4French giants Bordeaux relegated to sixth tier for financial irregularities\n\n- list 4 of 4UK, US, Netherlands warn of Iran-linked spyware\n\nAt least 300 shops of Intermarche or its Netto brand were targeted, along with more than 100 Cooperative U outlets, the retailers told the AFP news agency.\n\nCarrefour said 80 supermarkets were affected, while Monoprix said about 5 percent of its roughly 490 stores were hit. E Leclerc said several of its stores were targeted.\n\nThe Paris prosecutor’s office said the “large-scale” attack had affected “connected refrigerators in several major food retailers across France”. It led to food losses and fires, the office said.\n\nThe attack prompted Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu to order France’s National Cybersecurity Agency (ANSSI) to investigate, a person in his office told the Reuters news agency.\n\nThe source said France was coordinating with European counterparts because other countries could also be affected, adding that authorities were assessing whether other French companies were at risk.\n\n‘Unprecedented’ attack\n\nThere was no immediate estimate of industry-wide losses, but Intermarche CEO Thierry Cotillard told BFM TV the “unprecedented” attack would represent losses amounting to “tens of millions of euros nationwide in a single day”.\n\nAccording to BFM TV, the hackers “managed to remotely trigger the forced defrosting of the refrigeration systems in numerous stores”. The action resulted in the “destruction of stocks of fresh and frozen products”, it said.\n\nIntermarche told AFP that affected stores would have to “throw away all of the products” that were fresh or frozen.\n\nDominique Schelcher, chief executive of Cooperative U, said the attack had disrupted refrigeration systems since Sunday night.\n\n“Targeting equipment essential to the preservation of fresh products and to the food supply of the French people is extremely serious and is unprecedented. It is unacceptable,” he wrote on X.\n\nHe said the company’s teams were working to restore normal operations as quickly as possible, and that an investigation would be needed to “shed full light on this attack”.\n\nSupermarket cold aisles are typically run by control panels that manage an entire store’s fridges and freezers and can be accessed over the internet, which lets maintenance companies adjust them from anywhere.\n\nThe source close to Lecornu said ANSSI had identified a vulnerability in software supplied by Danish company Danfoss.\n\nDanfoss said it was “aware of incidents in France affecting a limited number of refrigeration management systems”.\n\nThe affected units “were running outdated firmware”, the company said, adding that it had “long advised customers” to keep their systems updated and replace older equipment.\n\n‘A global wake-up call’\n\nThe attack came after cybersecurity firm Claroty published research on August 9 describing vulnerabilities in Danfoss systems that could allow attackers to bypass authentication.\n\nFrance’s retail federation, known as the FCD, called on Danfoss to provide “full transparency”.\n\nIt said some affected stores were already operating again.\n\nFelix Aime, a senior cyberthreat intelligence analyst at Sekoia Threat Detection and Research, told Reuters that Spain, Portugal, Poland, Germany and Belgium had also been affected.\n\nSteven Lachance, a cybersecurity analyst, told Al Jazeera the attack was not unprecedented, though it was rare in its scale. He said that once a vulnerability is found in internet-connected equipment, cyber-attackers can log in remotely and trigger its features.\n\n“We have connected infrastructure that is live on the internet, and that can be hacked. It’s a new reality,” he said, describing the attack as “a global wake-up call”.\n\n“We absolutely need national and international measures to prevent these attacks, because otherwise it’s just going to keep happening,” he said.\n\nWhile retailers had lost millions of euros in food, the next target could be worse, Lachance warned. “It could target energy infrastructure or hospitals,” he said.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/13/unprecedented-cyberattack-hits-french-supermarkets-causing-food-losses?traffic_source=rss",
    time: "6 ore fa",
    pub_ts: 1791859030,
    published_at: "2026-10-13T02:37:10+00:00",
    language: "en",
    score: 1,
